[38;5;252m[1m## Three things worth knowing[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mstbds_make_hash_index[0m never initializes [38;5;10mtemp_key[0m, so comparing it unconditionally reads uninitialized heap. It is excluded from the general state comparison and checked only immediately after a string-mode [38;5;10mhmput_key[0m, which is where C defines it.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mshputs[0m on an [3mexisting[23m key aborts the C library on its own [38;5;10massert(b->index[i] == final_index)[0m: the macro reads [38;5;10mstbds_temp_key[0m, which [38;5;10mstbds_hmput_key[0m only refreshes in its first probe loop, so a wrap-around match leaves it stale and corrupts the table. The Rust port reproduces that asymmetry faithfully; the test restricts [38;5;10mshputs[0m to new keys, which is what [38;5;10mstr_put[0m itself does.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two paths are inherently uncomparable across two independent libraries because their outcome depends on heap addresses: a [38;5;10mSH_NONE[0m table used with [38;5;10mHM_STRING[0m, and a [38;5;10mSH_STRDUP[0m/[38;5;10mSH_ARENA[0m table used with [38;5;10mHM_BINARY[0m. Both make C [38;5;10mmemcmp[0m/[38;5;10mstrcmp[0m a caller string against a heap pointer. I confirmed by reading the source that the Rust branch structure is identical there, but did not test it.[0m[0m
